 -- Bibliograph Maintainers <maintainers@olamaelcu.net>  Mon, 02 Nov 2026 15:07:41 -0500

bibliograph (0.1.21) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Run the graphile task lists inside `bibliograph-worker.service`, retiring
    `bibliograph-jobs.service`. On a single-core host with under 1 GB of RAM the
    second Node runtime — 15 MB resident before the tsx loader — was memory the
    web app needed; the two lists keep their separate pools and concurrency
    settings (`GRAPHILE_WORKER_CONCURRENCY_SEARCH` / `_TAP`), they just share one
    process and one event loop with the TAP consumer now.
  * Sharing a process does not share a fate: a TAP subscription that ends is
    retried after 30 s while the task lists keep running. TAP goes away whenever
    its host is under pressure, and a dead subscription must not stop job
    processing — the same coupling would otherwise turn a TAP outage into a
    stalled ingest queue.
  * Upgrades clean up after themselves: the postinst stops a still-running
    `bibliograph-jobs` and removes its enable symlink, since dpkg only drops the
    unit file. `deploy.sh` no longer restarts or verifies the retired unit.

 -- Bibliograph Maintainers <maintainers@olamaelcu.net>  Mon, 02 Nov 2026 13:52:11 -0500

bibliograph (0.1.20) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Unstick the TAP consumer. Ingestion stalled for 14 minutes on 2026-11-02
    with 2,863 events sitting in TAP's outbox and `retrying timed out event`
    every two minutes: acks were created for a whole batch up front and then
    awaited after the queue write, while `@atcute/tap` drains its ack queue as
    more messages arrive — so the read loop parked waiting on acks the server
    would only send once the loop kept reading.
  * Acks are now queued from the read loop and never awaited. They are still
    only issued after the batch is durably in `graphile_worker`, so the
    at-least-once handoff is unchanged; an ack lost to a crash just means TAP
    redelivers, and every upsert is keyed by uri.
  * A failed enqueue no longer acks. The old path logged the failure and then
    awaited the same acks, which acknowledged TAP for records it had dropped —
    silent loss with no dead-letter trail. Now the batch stays unacked so TAP
    redelivers it.
  * Flush on a timer instead of only on the next event. A quiet tail left the
    last events buffered until TAP's outbox timed out, which turned a one-event
    trickle into a permanent two-minute retry loop.

  * Batches no longer dead-letter on a repeated uri. One `INSERT … ON CONFLICT
    DO UPDATE` that names the same uri twice is rejected for the whole
    statement (`cannot affect row a second time`), which is what the 778 rows in
    `tap_dead_letter` were — a single redelivered event took every record in its
    batch with it. The consumer's buffer and the upsert task now collapse to one
    write per uri, last event wins, and every collapsed event is still acked.
  * A delete is confirmed against the record's own repo before the mirror row
    goes. Deletes and upserts for the same record are separate jobs on separate
    workers, so a late upsert could resurrect a removed row — the mirror held
    2,845 `bookShelving` rows against 2,839 in the repo. When the repo still
    serves the record, the row is mirrored instead of deleted; when the repo
    cannot be asked, the job retries rather than guess.
  * Ingestion health is visible on `/metrics`: `tap_ack_failures_total`,
    `tap_enqueue_failures_total`, `tap_duplicate_events_total`. Failing acks were
    the symptom of the stall and left no trace anywhere.
  * The queue-depth check reuses one Postgres pool instead of opening and
    closing one per read, from inside the read loop it is measuring.

  * Backpressure is measured where events actually wait. The consumer now reads
    TAP's `/stats/outbox-buffer` (exported as `tap_outbox_buffer` and
    `tap_resync_buffer`) and gates on the larger of that and our own job
    count; the outbox held 2,863 events through the stall and nothing surfaced
    it. The job-count read stays as the fallback for a TAP build without stats,
    so an unavailable reading never reads as "no backlog".
  * Note for the next attempt at resuming: this TAP build has no cursor. Its
    `/channel` handler reads no query parameters at all — a bogus `cursor` on
    the websocket URL connects happily — and the routes are health, channel,
    repos, resolve, info and stats. Resume is the outbox plus unacknowledged
    events, which is what the ack-after-durable-enqueue rule already relies on.

